#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481727} :activity/id #uuid "5cff95fc-6f6c-5d25-ae4b-a780b9efb5c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427258} :answer-field/id #uuid "96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077" :question/math-academy-id "q-343675" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is $4,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4166c13b-ac53-51f9-9738-1fcaff73fb79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10f3398-95fb-51ac-819d-349fa3583218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462315} {:answer/id #uuid "93d77f90-d255-502e-a3d2-a8904ab3576f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a3385f-3cab-5129-83bb-f7a582ea68df" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a268ac-7559-5f46-bf6c-3d1043ac53f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462317} {:answer/id #uuid "47e07c54-ee13-5022-a287-d6f6ab82ff3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462317} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b82a872-8326-5513-8f4a-ccff55e33bb4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa10a572-2a12-5313-a511-b89c8f024781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462319} {:answer/id #uuid "f79ee182-a849-5b22-a888-28a5c0d2851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462319} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc60cb7-562c-5b04-bbc2-febf547f3bde" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28a570c8-4263-5a25-a7a9-87fb602a6de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343490" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly solution I matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "49ae1a21-0dd4-5aa8-8ab3-c3f8d983ec48" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8588f05c-f259-5f6f-95a4-ba743efb8633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230323} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2a5ac-5e6b-5176-ada3-76165f3827b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230324} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f684b-31be-53dd-959a-4c693200fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230325} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbe1595-b0a0-5005-89d3-b1a66671386d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad71e63-ab40-5319-b40d-20fec2c9d4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c1fc5b-ecb6-509b-ab8e-ba64d464ed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2dc0571-7d63-58c4-a7e9-b264e1dac3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343760" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "60cce0c1-556a-5961-8f98-6d5844f01954"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ca90e4-8cb7-55b8-a354-4e9dc097fb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355277} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd7fbc0-1387-5d34-8f46-c4279237b187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355354} {:answer/id #uuid "80d2b946-bf3f-50ed-94d6-6429fb7fcc6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355796} {:answer/id #uuid "93972c70-73ca-5e80-9504-616aadd0051d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355966} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c23ba-58b1-5366-a8c3-d53ce428b496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356647} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae144dc-0f56-5164-b0eb-64f56e70b5d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07acc17d-166d-54d0-8f9f-eb0556f3a46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94235" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=7\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{7}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{7}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{7}.$"} :step/id #uuid "abd504bd-fe3b-56bf-9421-c152e2c9477e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaa23f1-3d6f-5700-8adf-0ab757d01cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b00e565-5dc1-5b1f-8a94-9d5cc2730e81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343699" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af750ac2-1d2a-5385-920d-12fa70467b02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481702}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481572}]}
